      "text": "## Bottom line\n\n**This is a good offer, but not a compelling \u201cleave a job you like\u201d offer. I would negotiate rather than accept or walk away immediately.**\n\nMy recommended opening ask:\n\n- **Base:** $260,000\n- **New-hire equity:** **$1.4 million**\n- **Sign-on:** **$150,000**, or a documented make-whole amount based on what you forfeit at Meta\n\nA reasonable landing zone would be approximately:\n\n- **$250,000\u2013$255,000 base**\n- **$1.25 million\u2013$1.35 million equity**\n- **$100,000\u2013$150,000 sign-on/make-whole**\n\nIf Google cannot get close, staying at Meta is likely the financially rational choice\u2014especially if your forward-looking Meta compensation is already around $600,000 or more.\n\n---\n\n## What the offer is actually worth\n\nOn a simple four-year annualized basis:\n\n| Component | Annual value |\n|---|---:|\n| Base | $235,000 |\n| Target bonus | $47,000 |\n| Equity | $270,000 |\n| **Annualized target compensation** | **$552,000** |\n\nYour base is about 59% of the way through the posted $183,000\u2013$271,000 range. It is not a lowball base, but there is room above it.\n\nRecent self-reported data puts Google L6 PM compensation in the Bay Area around **$606,000 median total compensation**, though another recent U.S.-wide analysis puts L6 PM median TC closer to **$514,000**. These datasets mix new offers and existing employees, and can include refresh grants and stock movement, so they should be treated as directional rather than exact. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/companies/google/salaries/product-manager/locations/san-francisco-bay-area?utm_source=openai))\n\nFor comparison, reported Meta L6 PM compensation is roughly **$578,000\u2013$595,000**, again with the same crowdsourced-data caveats. ([scoutify.com](https://scoutify.com/companies/meta/?utm_source=openai))\n\nSo the Google package is:\n\n- **Within the broad L6 market**\n- Probably **below the Bay Area Google L6 midpoint**\n- Roughly comparable to, or somewhat below, typical Meta IC6 compensation\n- **Not enough of a premium for a happy passive candidate**\n\n---\n\n## Watch the vesting schedule\n\nAsk the recruiter to confirm the exact vesting percentages in writing. Google commonly uses a front-loaded schedule of **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**, though other schedules have been reported. ([thesimplemoney.com](https://thesimplemoney.com/employers/google/gsu-grants-and-vesting-schedule?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf yours is 38/32/20/10, the offer looks like this before refreshes and changes in Alphabet\u2019s stock price:\n\n| Year | Base + target bonus | New-hire equity | Target TC |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | $282,000 | $410,400 | **$692,400** |\n| 2 | $282,000 | $345,600 | **$627,600** |\n| 3 | $282,000 | $216,000 | **$498,000** |\n| 4 | $282,000 | $108,000 | **$390,000** |\n\nThat is a substantial compensation cliff.\n\nGoogle does have an annual process that considers salary, bonus and equity refresh awards, with performance, location, role and manager discretion among the inputs. But refreshes are discretionary, exactly as your recruiter said. ([services.google.com](https://services.google.com/fh/files/blogs/pay_equity_info_sheet.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\nTherefore:\n\n> **Do not value unspecified future refreshes as though they are guaranteed compensation.**\n\nYou can model them as upside, but the initial grant should be sufficient to justify the move on its own.\n\n---\n\n## What I would negotiate\n\n### 1. Equity: ask for $1.4 million\n\nThis should be your primary recurring-compensation ask.\n\n- Current: **$1.08 million**\n- Ask: **$1.4 million**\n- Reasonable landing point: **$1.25 million\u2013$1.35 million**\n\nAt $1.3 million, annualized equity becomes $325,000, and total annualized target compensation becomes approximately:\n\n- $255,000 base\n- $51,000 target bonus\n- $325,000 equity\n- **$631,000 annualized TC**\n\nAt the full $260,000 base and $1.4 million equity ask, annualized target compensation would be about **$662,000**.\n\nThat is a more appropriate package for persuading a happy Meta IC6 to make a lateral-level move.\n\n### 2. Base: ask for $260,000\n\nThe posted maximum is $271,000, and recent reported L6 PM base compensation is around $240,000. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/companies/google/salaries/product-manager/locations/san-francisco-bay-area?utm_source=openai))\n\nI would:\n\n- Ask for **$260,000**\n- Expect that **$250,000\u2013$255,000** may be the realistic result\n- Not make base the sole sticking point, because equity has greater dollar leverage\n\nIncreasing base also raises your target bonus:\n\n- $250,000 base \u2192 $50,000 target bonus\n- $255,000 base \u2192 $51,000\n- $260,000 base \u2192 $52,000\n\n### 3. Sign-on: ask for $150,000 or an exact make-whole\n\nNo sign-on is the clearest weakness, particularly because Google recruited you away from Meta.\n\nCalculate what you will actually forfeit by leaving:\n\n- Meta RSUs scheduled to vest over the next 12 months\n- Any annual bonus you lose because of timing\n- Any refresh grant already awarded but not vested\n- Any compensation-review adjustment you would otherwise receive\n- Any other cash payment with a known eligibility date\n\nThen ask Google to make you whole.\n\nIf the forfeiture is:\n\n- $80,000, ask for at least $80,000\u2013$100,000\n- $125,000, ask for $125,000\u2013$150,000\n- $200,000, ask for $200,000\n\nIf your calculated loss is lower or uncertain, **$150,000 is a defensible opening anchor** given your position, though Google may counter below it.\n\n---\n\n## Suggested script\n\nYou could say:\n\n> I\u2019m excited about the role and the team. At the same time, I\u2019m happy at Meta and wasn\u2019t actively looking, so the package needs to clearly justify the financial and execution risk of moving.\n>\n> Looking at my current compensation, the equity and bonus I would forfeit, and the four-year value of the Google package\u2014particularly the decline in the later years\u2014I was hoping we could get to a $260,000 base, a $1.4 million new-hire GSU grant, and a $150,000 sign-on bonus.\n>\n> I\u2019m flexible about the mix if there are constraints on individual components, but I would need the overall guaranteed offer value to move meaningfully higher. The sign-on is especially important as a make-whole for compensation I would leave behind at Meta.\n\nIf you want to give a firmer closing target:\n\n> If we could reach approximately $255,000 base, $1.3 million in equity, and a $150,000 sign-on, I would feel much more comfortable making the move.\n\nOnly say that you will sign immediately at a particular package if you genuinely will.\n\n---\n\n## Your decision rule\n\nBuild a comparison using **forward compensation**, not last year\u2019s W-2 and not the original value of your Meta grants.\n\n### Stay at Meta value\n\nFor each of the next four years, estimate:\n\n- Current base\n- Expected target bonus\n- Scheduled vesting from all existing grants\n- Already-awarded refreshes\n- A conservative estimate of future refreshes\u2014or zero, for a strict comparison\n\nUse Meta\u2019s current stock price consistently for all unvested shares. Do not count past appreciation twice.\n\n### Google value\n\nInclude:\n\n- Base\n- Target bonus\n- Exact new-hire vesting by year\n- Sign-on\n- **Zero refreshes in the conservative case**\n- A separate scenario with reasonable refreshes, but not as the primary case\n\nAlso account for the switching costs that compensation tables miss:\n\n- Giving up known performance credibility and internal network\n- A new performance system and manager\n- Risk that the role\u2019s scope is weaker than represented\n- Commute/location differences\n- Promotion trajectory\n- Reorganization or team-matching risk\n\nBecause you are happy and were not looking, I would generally require at least a **10%\u201315% risk-adjusted premium** over your expected Meta compensation, or a genuinely superior role that compensates for a smaller financial premium.\n\n---\n\n## Accept, negotiate, or walk?\n\n### My recommendation: **negotiate**\n\nI would not accept the initial offer as-is. You have unusually strong leverage:\n\n- You are currently employed at the same broad level at another top-paying company.\n- You are happy and can credibly stay.\n- Google initiated the conversation.\n- Google has already spent the time to interview, level and select you.\n- The offer has no make-whole payment.\n- The base is well below the posted maximum.\n- The later-year equity cliff creates a legitimate concern.\n\n### Accept after negotiation if:\n\n- The job is materially better, **and**\n- Google gets the package to roughly **$625,000\u2013$650,000 annualized**, with an appropriate sign-on for your actual Meta forfeiture\n\n### Probably stay at Meta if:\n\n- Your forward Meta compensation is already **$600,000\u2013$650,000+**\n- Google stays near $552,000 annualized\n- Google will not make you whole for near-term vesting\n- The role is merely \u201cinteresting\u201d rather than a clear scope, manager or career upgrade\n\n### Walk away if:\n\n- Google barely moves\n- The recruiter asks you to rely heavily on future refreshes\n- Your Meta four-year package is equal or better\n- You do not see a clear nonfinancial advantage\n\nIn short: **good market-level offer, inadequate switching premium. Ask for $260K / $1.4M / $150K, aim to land around $255K / $1.3M / $100K\u2013$150K, and stay at Meta if Google cannot close the gap with your actual forward compensation.**",
